    "text": "The brehon was an Irish judge. The brehon belonged to the class of druids. The name comes from the Irish brithemain. That word means judge. Ancient brehons did not evaluate cases. They did not enforce the law. Both duties were the king's prerogative. Brehons used precognitive skill. They drew on memorized history and poetry. This memory came from years of training. Spiritual powers bound them to be just. The great judge Sencha broke out in blotches. This happened when he gave a biased judgment about women. Certain brehons wore distinctive clothing and ornaments. Morann wore a chain around his neck. The chain tightened if he spoke a false It grew loose when he spoke a true judgment. Many women brehons gained renown. The great Brigit was an honored woman brehon. She healed Sencha's blotched face. She did so by correcting his bias against women.",
    "okr": {
        "objective": "Describe the ancient Irish brehon and the spiritual bond to just judgment.",
